    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "1940366600554844306",
      "url": "https://x.com/iMPACTPH2019/status/1940366600554844306",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/iMPACTPH2019/status/1940366600554844306",
      "text": "EDCOM 2 LAUNCHES 'BAYANG BUMABASA' CHALLENGE TO RALLY MAYORS BEHIND LITERACY PUSH\n\nThe Second Congressional Commission on Education (EDCOM 2) officially launched the nationwide \"Bayang Bumabasa: Mayors for Literacy\" social media video challenge on Tuesday, calling on local chief executives to champion foundational literacy in their communities.\n\nTimed with the first day in office of newly elected and re-elected mayors, the campaign urges local leaders to place literacy at the top of their education agenda. Through the challenge, mayors are invited to post short videos showcasing their cities' literacy initiatives, best practices, and commitments to ensuring that \"Bawat Bata Makakabasa, Para sa Bayang Bumabasa!\"\n\nQuezon City Mayor Joy Belmonte is the first to accept the challenge from EDCOM 2 and is set to release her video next week.\n\nEDCOM 2 Co-Chair and Pasig City Lone District Rep. Roman Romulo emphasized the importance of local government involvement in learning recovery, especially in light of troubling data from the Comprehensive Rapid Literacy Assessment, which found that over 2.85 million Grade 1 to 3 students—or 52.26%—are not yet reading at grade level.\n\n\"LGUs should really be involved. DILG should help out. Let's lessen the burden on teachers and schools. If we truly want to reach children who struggle with reading, there needs to be coordination from the barangay to the city level. Literacy should be everyone's agenda, not just DepEd's,\" Romulo said during a recent field visit.\n\nEducation Secretary Sonny Angara echoed this sentiment, noting, \"Improving the quality of education is a marching order from the President, and we believe LGUs have a critical role to play. If we can scale the kind of commitment and innovation we've seen in some of the top-performing LGUs, we can lift learning outcomes across the country.\"\n\nEDCOM 2 encourages the public to follow the campaign online and track how mayors respond, using official hashtags to amplify and support local efforts. The initiative forms part of EDCOM 2's broader mandate to review and improve the Philippine education system through collaborative, multi-sectoral action.\n\n📷 EDCOM 2",
